\section{Ledger Verification and Integrity Guard}

The QCSE-X ledger is a sequential, tamper-evident chain of encrypted event 
blocks. Each block records a significant physical or engine-level transition 
and is cryptographically bound to its predecessor. All verification is 
performed homomorphically without decrypting any ledger contents.

\subsection{Block Construction}
Let $E_k$ denote the encrypted event payload at step $k$. The corresponding 
ledger block is:
\[
B_k = \mathcal{H}(E_k \Vert B_{k-1}),
\]
where $\mathcal{H}$ is a cryptographic hash function applied to encrypted 
inputs, and $\Vert$ denotes concatenation.

The genesis block $B_0$ is a fixed encrypted constant.

\subsection{Chain Validity}
A ledger of $N$ blocks is valid if:
\[
\forall k \in \{1,\dots,N\},\quad 
B_k = \mathcal{H}(E_k \Vert B_{k-1}).
\]

The Integrity Guard evaluates this condition homomorphically:
\[
V_k = 
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } B_k = \mathcal{H}(E_k \Vert B_{k-1}),\\[6pt]
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

The chain is accepted if:
\[
\prod_{k=1}^{N} V_k = 1.
\]

\subsection{Tamper Detection}
Three classes of tampering are considered:

\paragraph{Altered Block}
If $B_k$ is modified, then:
\[
\mathcal{H}(E_k \Vert B_{k-1}) \neq B_k,
\]
yielding $V_k = 0$.

\paragraph{Missing Block}
If block $B_j$ is removed, the chain condition fails at $B_{j+1}$:
\[
\mathcal{H}(E_{j+1} \Vert B_{j}) \text{ is undefined}.
\]

\paragraph{Reordered Chain}
If blocks $B_i$ and $B_j$ are swapped ($i < j$), then:
\[
\mathcal{H}(E_j \Vert B_{i-1}) \neq B_i,
\]
and the chain collapses.

\subsection{Encrypted Self-Proof Module}
The ledger includes a self-proof vector:
\[
P = \left( \mathcal{H}(B_1), \mathcal{H}(B_2), \dots, \mathcal{H}(B_N) \right),
\]
stored entirely in encrypted form.

Verification is performed by checking:
\[
\mathcal{H}(B_k) = P_k \quad \forall k.
\]

\subsection{Sovereign Integrity Condition}
QCSE-X declares sovereign integrity if and only if:
\[
\left( \prod_{k=1}^{N} V_k \right)
\cdot
\left( \prod_{k=1}^{N} \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{H}(B_k) = P_k} \right)
= 1,
\]
where $\mathbf{1}_{\text{condition}}$ is the encrypted indicator function.

If the condition evaluates to $0$, the system records a Sovereign Fault.